From: dandelion port <none@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] mtk_battery: do not force DEBUG logging when booted from recovery

This port boots from the `recovery` partition and always will: p33 holds stock
Android and is the way back, so p2 is the only partition this port writes.
MediaTek's gauge driver reads that as a recovery console and turns its logging
all the way up.

    drivers/power/supply/mediatek/battery/mtk_battery.c:205
        bool is_recovery_mode(void)
        {
                int boot_mode = get_boot_mode();
                ...
                if (boot_mode == RECOVERY_BOOT) {
                        gm.log_level = BMLOG_DEBUG_LEVEL;
                        fg_cust_data.daemon_log_level = BMLOG_DEBUG_LEVEL;

BM_DAEMON_DEFAULT_LOG_LEVEL is 3 (BMLOG_ERROR_LEVEL) and BMLOG_DEBUG_LEVEL is 7,
so this is not a nudge: every bm_debug(), bm_info() and bm_notice() in the gauge
and charger drivers starts printing, each one a pr_notice() into the kernel ring
buffer.  Measured on dandelion, six hours after a boot:

    journalctl --disk-usage                            907.6M
    lines in the journal for that boot                 872463
    of them kernel                                     380894
    kernel lines per second, steady state              ~17

and the twelve most frequent kernel messages were all this driver:

    31097  [fgauge_read_current] final current=... (ratio=...)
    11995  [force_get_tbat] t:... precise:...
    11995  [force_get_tbat] ...
    11460  [BattVoltToTemp] ...
     9478  [fgauge_read_current] final current=... (ratio=...)
     9114  [bq25601]:bq25601_dump_regs: Reg[..] = 0x..

The sysfs attribute that exists for this does not help.  Writing 3 to
/sys/devices/platform/battery/FG_daemon_log_level does set gm.log_level, and
is_recovery_mode() sets it straight back: it is called from the gauge's polling
paths, not once at probe, so the attribute reads 7 again within a second.  That
was measured too, on the running device, before writing this patch.

Dropping the two assignments leaves is_recovery_mode() answering the question its
name asks and nothing else.  Every other caller of it is unaffected, and the log
level stays at the driver's own default, which is what a device booted from
`boot` would have had.

Upstream is not a consideration: this is a 2019 vendor tree.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/mediatek/battery/mtk_battery.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/mediatek/battery/mtk_battery.c
@@ -211,11 +211,8 @@
 		return false;
 
 	bm_debug("mtk_battery boot mode =%d\n", boot_mode);
-	if (boot_mode == RECOVERY_BOOT) {
-		gm.log_level = BMLOG_DEBUG_LEVEL;
-		fg_cust_data.daemon_log_level = BMLOG_DEBUG_LEVEL;
+	if (boot_mode == RECOVERY_BOOT)
 		return true;
-	}
 
 	return false;
 }
